---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data.mjs";
const path = "/guides/custom-stand-up-pouch-specification/";
const title = "Custom Stand-Up Pouch Guide: Size, Materials and Artwork";
const schema = { "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "Article", headline: title, author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Welltroy" }, publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Welltroy" }, mainEntityOfPage: `${site.origin}${path}`, datePublished: "2026-10-24", dateModified: "2026-10-24" };
const breadcrumbs = { "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", itemListElement: [{ "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.origin }, { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Buying Guide", item: `${site.origin}/guides/choose-a-mailer/` }, { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Custom stand-up pouch specification", item: `${site.origin}${path}` }] };
---
<Layout title={`${title} | Welltroy`} description="Choose a custom stand-up pouch by testing filled capacity, bottom gusset, material barrier, closure and print artwork. Use a clear quote brief and approve a physical sample." canonicalPath={path} schemas={[schema, breadcrumbs]}>
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides/choose-a-mailer/">Buying Guide</a> / Custom stand-up pouches</div><p class="eyebrow">Flexible packaging buying guide</p><h1>{title}</h1><p class="lead">Start with what goes inside and how it is filled, sealed, stored and sold. A pouch photograph or flat size cannot establish usable capacity, barrier performance, food suitability or recyclability. Agree on the complete structure, then approve a filled sample before production.</p><nav class="anchor-links" aria-label="In this guide"><a href="#size">Size</a><a href="#structure">Materials</a><a href="#features">Features</a><a href="#artwork">Artwork</a><a href="#quote">Quote brief</a></nav></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container article-layout"><article class="article-main">
    <h2 id="size">Measure the Pouch and Test the Fill</h2>
    <p>Record width, total height and bottom gusset on a dieline. Ask whether the gusset dimension means the folded depth or the fully opened base, and where the measurement starts and ends. A zipper, top seal and side seals reduce usable space. Provide the intended fill weight or volume, product density, required headspace and the way the pouch must stand on a shelf. Fill and seal a physical sample with the real product before approving the final dimensions.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/products/stand-up-pouches/">catalogue stand-up pouch range</a> lists eight dimension references. These can shortlist a format; they are not capacity ratings. The supplier's illustrated 7 × 11 + 8 inch diagram explains measurement positions but is not a confirmed standard size or proof of fill volume.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Stand-up pouch specification decisions"><table><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Information to provide</th><th>Approval check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Size and bottom</td><td>Filled product dimensions, weight or volume, headspace and shelf stance</td><td>Usable opening, base shape and filled sample</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Material and barrier</td><td>Contents, oxygen/moisture/light sensitivity, storage and target shelf life</td><td>Exact layer structure, seals and relevant test data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Closure and features</td><td>Zipper, tear notch, window, valve, spout or handle if needed</td><td>Compatibility, placement and performance on the selected construction</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Print and finish</td><td>Dieline, artwork versions, colors and finish reference</td><td>Proof on the chosen material and a physical sample where critical</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h2 id="structure">Choose Materials for the Contents</h2>
    <p>The supplier sheets show outer print films, middle barrier options and inner sealing films as examples, including multi-layer structures. Those names do not specify a finished pouch. Ask for the exact layer sequence and thickness, sealing layer, adhesives and inks for the quoted construction. Match barrier and seal requirements to the contents, filling temperature, storage conditions and target shelf life. Heated filling or retort use needs a separately validated structure and process.</p>
    <p>Food-contact suitability depends on the complete pouch and the destination market. Ask for documents that apply to the exact selected structure and intended food use. The word “recyclable” on a supplier image is not evidence that every laminate, window or closure is recyclable; confirm the complete material and the relevant local collection route before making a claim.</p>
    <h2 id="features">Select Only the Features the Product Needs</h2>
    <p>A zipper can support repeated opening, a tear notch can help first opening, and a window can reveal contents. A valve, spout or handle changes the construction more substantially. The supplied images also show matte, gloss, soft-touch, holographic, spot UV and foil examples. Ask which combinations are feasible for the pouch size, material, print process and order volume; specialty finishes or a window may affect barrier, sealing and cost.</p>
    <p>For dry snacks, see <a href="/products/printed-snack-pouches/">Printed Snack Pouches</a>. For coordinated nut artwork versions, see <a href="/products/white-nut-pouches/">White Printed Nut Pouches</a>. The <a href="/products/custom-stand-up-pouches/">Custom Stand Up Pouches</a> range covers a broader made-to-order brief.</p>
    <h2 id="artwork">Prepare Front, Back and Gusset Artwork</h2>
    <p>Request the approved dieline before final design. Mark the front, back, bottom gusset, zipper, top seal, side seals, cut marks and any window or accessory. Keep essential text and barcodes clear of folds and seals. Editable AI or EPS files, or a production-ready PDF, are useful starting points; ask the production team to confirm the accepted format, fonts, bleed, safe area and color references. The sheet also mentions PSD and CorelDRAW, but file acceptance and any conversion should be checked for the chosen print process.</p>
    <p>Digital and gravure printing are shown as possible workflows, not interchangeable promises. Share the number of designs and quantity per design, then compare proof method, setup, color control and finish. Use the <a href="/guides/logo-artwork-checklist/">logo artwork checklist</a> to prepare source files and approval questions.</p>
    <h2 id="quote">Send a Brief That Can Be Quoted</h2>
    <ol><li>Describe the contents, fill weight or volume, filling method, storage and target shelf life.</li><li>Give desired width, total height and bottom gusset—or ask for a sample recommendation based on the real filled product.</li><li>State required barrier, destination market and any food-contact or recycling documentation.</li><li>List zipper, notch, window and other features, plus finish and print preferences.</li><li>Attach front, back and gusset artwork or a design reference; give quantity per size and artwork version.</li><li>Ask for a written specification, dieline, proof, filled-sample check, quotation and lead time before production.</li></ol>
    <a class="button" href="/quote/?product=custom-stand-up-pouches#quote-form">Request a custom pouch quote</a>
  </article><aside class="side-card"><p class="eyebrow">Compare pouch ranges</p><h2>Related Products</h2><div class="guide-options"><a href="/products/custom-stand-up-pouches/"><strong>Custom Stand Up Pouches</strong><span>Made-to-order construction, print and features</span></a><a href="/products/stand-up-pouches/"><strong>Stand-up Pouches</strong><span>Eight catalogue dimension references</span></a><a href="/products/printed-snack-pouches/"><strong>Printed Snack Pouches</strong><span>Dry snack presentation and barrier brief</span></a><a href="/products/white-nut-pouches/"><strong>White Printed Nut Pouches</strong><span>Coordinated artwork versions for nut ranges</span></a></div></aside></div></section>
</Layout>
